// KNOWN LIMITATION — should verify, currently rejected.
//
// The induction form of the same gap. While `f` is verified, the post of its
// recursive call `f(next(x))` is the induction hypothesis, released by the
// batch's post rule behind the call's `f%pre` token. The token is assumed under
// the call's path condition `!(x == 0)`, so the hypothesis `f(next(x)) == 0` holds
// only there. Proving the exit assert `ite(x == 0, 0, f(next(x))) == 0` then needs
// a case split on `x == 0`: the `x == 0` arm folds, the other arm goes through the
// hypothesis.
//
// That the precondition `ok(next(x))` holds for *every* `x` (the axiom) does not
// help: the hypothesis is gated by the token, not by the precondition. It used to
// verify only because the post rule replayed the hypothesis with no gate at all,
// which made Helium prove `false` from a recursive call on a dead branch
// (`failing/functions/limited_twin_post_dead_branch.vpr`,
// `failing/functions/recursive_post_dead_branch_body.vpr`). With an unconditional
// recursive call the hypothesis needs no split and the same proof goes through:
// `verify::declaration::e2e::recursive_function_postcondition_by_induction`.
//
// Root cause and rationale: see `goal_needs_case_split_heap_free.vpr`.
//
// Was `verify::declaration::e2e::recursive_function_postcondition_by_induction`,
// whose body was `x == 0 ? 0 : f(next(x))`.

domain D {
    function ok(x: Int): Bool
    function next(x: Int): Int
    axiom { forall x: Int :: {next(x)} ok(next(x)) }
}

function f(x: Int): Int
    requires ok(x)
    ensures result == 0
{ x == 0 ? 0 : f(next(x)) }
